<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Valve's bone pipeline, ported to match its ARCHITECTURE and not merely its output (D88).

    The owner, after the audit in docs/findings/35-the-bone-pipeline-audit.md found the old pose
    loop had diverged structurally rather than cosmetically: "all of the architecture needs to match
    valves", and "im going for the option that is 100% parity".

    **Why a project of its own rather than more files in Scene.** Two reasons, and the first is
    Valve's own layout:

      1. The SDK splits this in half and the halves have different natures. src/public/bone_setup.cpp
         is computation over a studio header - InitPose, AccumulatePose, CalcBoneAdj, CalcAutoplay-
         Sequences, the IK solver - and knows nothing about entities. game/client/c_baseanimating.cpp
         is the per-entity object that owns a bone cache and drives it. Mirrored here as the
         BoneSetup/ and Animating/ folders.

      2. Scene is where the 200-line loop lived, and the loop was 200 lines precisely because
         posing, lighting, logging and instance emission had no boundary between them. Giving the
         pipeline an assembly is what makes "the engine's stage list" checkable rather than a claim.

    **References Content and Logging, and NOTHING else - that restriction is the design.** Not Core,
    not Scene, not Render. Valve's bone setup has no notion of a demo, a tick or a scene prop, and
    neither does this: the parent link an entity follows is a property somebody else sets, the way
    C_BaseAnimating::GetMoveParent is. A reference added here should be treated as a design change
    needing a reason, not a convenience.

    **Plain net10.0, deliberately, and it is not a formality (B184).** Measured 2026-08-24: 115 of
    119 test files in Viewer3D.Tests need nothing from Windows, and Tf2DemoSalvage.Scene - which is
    itself net10.0 - has no Stryker config at all, because 18 of its pose-path test files live in
    that Windows-pinned project and mutation runs happen on Linux ARM boxes. So the densest
    behaviour in the renderer has never been mutation tested. This project ships with its own tests
    on net10.0 and its own Stryker config so it cannot inherit that.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Tf2DemoSalvage.Animation</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Tf2DemoSalvage.Animation.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Parsed studio data: bones, sequences, blend grids, attachments. The equivalent of the
         CStudioHdr that every bone_setup.cpp entry point takes. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Content\Tf2DemoSalvage.Content.csproj" />

    <!-- ILogger, per D83. The pipeline reports under its own category so a pose question can be
         answered from a log rather than by re-running with a breakpoint. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Tf2DemoSalvage.Logging\Tf2DemoSalvage.Logging.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
